<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niccolò Paganini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1782">1782</a>–<a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niccolò Paganini was an Italian violinist and composer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, the most celebrated virtuoso of his age. From 1805 to 1809 he served at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa Bonaparte</a> in Lucca, before touring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and, from 1828, the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where his technique and stage presence made him a legend. His 24 Caprices for solo violin remain central to the instrument's repertoire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niccolo-paganini.jpg" alt="Niccolò Paganini" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Whittle (active in Preston, early 19th century) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paganini.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Niccolò Paganini was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> on 27 October 1782, the third of six children of Antonio and Teresa Paganini. His father was an unsuccessful ship chandler who supplemented his income as a musician and by selling mandolins. Paganini began on the mandolin at five and moved to the violin at seven. In Parma, Alessandro Rolla sent him to his own teacher Ferdinando Paer and later to Gasparo Ghiretti. He is thought to have developed his relationship with the guitar about 1796, and preferred to play it in intimate rather than public concerts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1801 Paganini became first violin of the Republic of Lucca, though much of his income came from freelancing. In 1805 Lucca was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa Bonaparte</a>, whose husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felice-baciocchi">Felice Baciocchi</a> he taught for ten years. He left the court late in 1809 to resume freelance work. A concert at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-scala">La Scala</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1813 was his first break. In 1827 Pope Leo XII gave him the Order of the Golden Spur.</p><p>His continental tour began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in August 1828 and took in every major city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> until February 1831 in Strasbourg, followed by tours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Theme and variations were his most popular pieces, and he also played modified works by Rodolphe Kreutzer and Giovanni Battista Viotti. He owned 11 Stradivari at his death.</p><p>He was diagnosed with syphilis as early as 1822, and his treatment with mercury and opium had serious side effects. He ended his concert career in September 1834. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> he published his compositions and violin methods rather than guarding their secrets, as was widely believed. In 1836 he set up a casino in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, whose immediate failure left him in financial ruin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Paganini left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1838 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, where his condition worsened. He refused the last rites when a priest was sent in May 1840, thinking them premature, and died on 27 May 1840 of internal haemorrhaging before another could be summoned. Because of this and his rumoured association with the devil, the Church denied him a Catholic burial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. It took four years and an appeal to the Pope for his body to be moved to Genoa, but it was not buried there. It was finally buried in a cemetery in Parma in 1876 and reinterred in a new cemetery there in 1896.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niccolò Paganini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>She patronised the violinist <b>Niccolò Paganini</b> at her court.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-lucca-and-piombino">Principality of Lucca and Piombino</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1819" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4764 593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Niccolò Paganini"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1819">What was here in 1819? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
